1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,064
Welcome to episode one of the LLD Workshop. LLD means low-level design. By the

2
00:00:05,064 --> 00:00:10,129
end you'll know what this round is, how it's scored, and a repeatable method

3
00:00:10,129 --> 00:00:14,469
that turns one vague sentence into a class diagram and working Java.

4
00:00:14,969 --> 00:00:19,858
First a complete tiny answer, a music playlist. Then the method, with every step

5
00:00:19,858 --> 00:00:24,746
applied to one running example: a parking lot, the most asked question in this

6
00:00:24,746 --> 00:00:25,095
round.

7
00:00:25,595 --> 00:00:30,214
Keep the chapter one notes open beside the video. When I say pause and

8
00:00:30,214 --> 00:00:31,204
think, really pause.

9
00:00:32,104 --> 00:00:38,067
Concept one. Imagine an interviewer says: design a parking lot. Then they stop talking.

10
00:00:38,067 --> 00:00:41,049
What are they actually asking you for?

11
00:00:41,949 --> 00:00:47,528
The low-level design round gives you one application to design while someone watches, usually

12
00:00:47,528 --> 00:00:53,108
in forty-five to sixty minutes. A parking lot, a vending machine, a chess game.

13
00:00:53,608 --> 00:00:58,454
You design the classes inside the program. A class is a named type holding

14
00:00:58,454 --> 00:01:03,300
data, called fields, and methods that work on it. An object is one instance

15
00:01:03,300 --> 00:01:04,339
with real values.

16
00:01:04,839 --> 00:01:10,232
A responsibility is the one job a class does, in a single sentence. A

17
00:01:10,232 --> 00:01:15,626
relationship is how two classes connect: one uses, holds, owns or extends the other.

18
00:01:16,126 --> 00:01:21,647
U-M-L, the Unified Modeling Language, is the standard set of symbols for drawing classes.

19
00:01:21,647 --> 00:01:26,379
A design pattern is a named, reusable answer to a recurring problem.

20
00:01:26,879 --> 00:01:32,282
Analogy: system design is the city plan, roads and power. Low-level design is the

21
00:01:32,282 --> 00:01:36,912
floor plan of one house: which rooms, and which doors connect them.

22
00:01:37,812 --> 00:01:45,475
Same product, two zoom levels. Low-level design stays inside one application. System design spans

23
00:01:45,475 --> 00:01:46,569
many machines.

24
00:01:47,069 --> 00:01:54,741
The vocabulary: classes, interfaces and patterns here, versus load balancers, databases, caches and queues

25
00:01:54,741 --> 00:01:55,289
there.

26
00:01:55,789 --> 00:02:01,977
The output: a class diagram plus code, versus an architecture diagram plus capacity numbers.

27
00:02:02,477 --> 00:02:07,517
The question: design the classes for a parking lot, versus a parking service for

28
00:02:07,517 --> 00:02:11,117
ten million users. Practising one gives little for the other.

29
00:02:12,017 --> 00:02:16,794
After this handbook you'll do four things. Turn design a parking lot into a

30
00:02:16,794 --> 00:02:19,182
bounded requirement list with an out-of-scope list.

31
00:02:19,682 --> 00:02:24,152
Pick classes with exactly one job each, and connect them correctly.

32
00:02:24,652 --> 00:02:27,461
Code the Java behind the core flows until it runs.

33
00:02:27,961 --> 00:02:33,241
And name the pattern you used, the one you rejected, and why. The reusable

34
00:02:33,241 --> 00:02:35,881
part is the method, not memorised answers.

35
00:02:36,781 --> 00:02:42,029
Concept two. This is for anyone facing this round, especially if you write object-oriented

36
00:02:42,029 --> 00:02:46,152
code daily but have never defended a class diagram out loud.

37
00:02:46,652 --> 00:02:52,111
You need one object-oriented language, enough that a class with a constructor, the method

38
00:02:52,111 --> 00:02:56,789
that sets up a new object, reads easily. Examples are Java twenty-one.

39
00:02:57,289 --> 00:03:02,899
You don't need U-M-L or system design. This round stays inside one running program.

40
00:03:03,399 --> 00:03:08,295
Basic data structures are enough. A list keeps order, a map looks up by

41
00:03:08,295 --> 00:03:13,192
key, a set has no duplicates, a queue is first in first out, a

42
00:03:13,192 --> 00:03:18,088
heap gives the smallest item fast. Maps and lists will be your in-memory storage.

43
00:03:18,988 --> 00:03:23,918
Concept three. The interviewer says one sentence and goes quiet. Nothing tells you how

44
00:03:23,918 --> 00:03:28,847
many classes, whether to code, or how long you have. That silence is the

45
00:03:28,847 --> 00:03:32,368
hard part, so let's learn the shape of the round.

46
00:03:33,268 --> 00:03:39,602
Two flavours. The object-oriented design round, O-O-D, runs forty-five to sixty minutes. Machine coding

47
00:03:39,602 --> 00:03:44,127
is about ninety minutes of coding, two hours with review.

48
00:03:44,627 --> 00:03:50,173
O-O-D happens on a whiteboard or shared doc. Machine coding happens in your own

49
00:03:50,173 --> 00:03:51,757
I-D-E, your code editor.

50
00:03:52,257 --> 00:03:58,321
O-O-D produces requirements, a class diagram and code for two or three flows. Machine

51
00:03:58,321 --> 00:04:00,920
coding produces a complete running program.

52
00:04:01,420 --> 00:04:07,051
Machine coding stores data in memory, in maps and lists, unless asked otherwise.

53
00:04:07,551 --> 00:04:13,799
O-O-D is a continuous conversation. Machine coding is mostly silent coding, then a demo.

54
00:04:14,299 --> 00:04:22,498
O-O-D judges scope, responsibilities and trade-offs. Machine coding judges working code, data models, interfaces

55
00:04:22,498 --> 00:04:23,669
and extensibility.

56
00:04:24,169 --> 00:04:30,218
The typical failures: talking without converging, versus not finishing or one giant class.

57
00:04:31,118 --> 00:04:38,086
Machine-coding words, with our parking lot. Data models hold the problem's data: Ticket, Spot.

58
00:04:38,586 --> 00:04:43,149
The service interface is the public methods a caller uses: park and exit.

59
00:04:43,649 --> 00:04:49,361
Extensibility: an electric vehicle type is a new value, not edits everywhere.

60
00:04:49,861 --> 00:04:55,329
Edge cases: an empty lot, a full lot, a negative time, a duplicate ticket.

61
00:04:55,829 --> 00:05:01,233
Thread safety: staying correct when two entry gates, two threads, run at once. A

62
00:05:01,233 --> 00:05:03,934
thread is an independent line of execution.

63
00:05:04,834 --> 00:05:10,867
Concept four, the heart of the method. Every answer follows five core steps, in

64
00:05:10,867 --> 00:05:16,899
this order. Clarify the requirements. Find the actors and use cases. Find the classes.

65
00:05:16,899 --> 00:05:19,485
Connect the classes. Write the code.

66
00:05:19,985 --> 00:05:24,476
Order matters more than speed. Skip step one, and in step three you'll invent

67
00:05:24,476 --> 00:05:28,968
classes no requirement asked for. Let's walk each step on the parking lot so

68
00:05:28,968 --> 00:05:30,251
you see it happen.

69
00:05:31,151 --> 00:05:37,058
Step one, clarify. For the parking lot: multiple floors, five spot types, pay at

70
00:05:37,058 --> 00:05:40,011
exit, refuse when full, about ten lines.

71
00:05:40,511 --> 00:05:45,780
Step two, actors and use cases. An actor is anyone outside who uses it,

72
00:05:45,780 --> 00:05:51,049
a use case is one thing they do. Customer takes a ticket. Admin adds

73
00:05:51,049 --> 00:05:51,801
a floor.

74
00:05:52,301 --> 00:05:59,349
Step three, classes, one job each: ParkingLot, Floor, Spot, Vehicle, Ticket, Rate.

75
00:05:59,849 --> 00:06:04,898
Step four, connect them. A lot owns floors, a floor owns spots, a ticket

76
00:06:04,898 --> 00:06:06,340
points at one spot.

77
00:06:06,840 --> 00:06:11,974
Step five, code the two or three main flows: park, exit and pricing. Not

78
00:06:11,974 --> 00:06:13,808
every class at full depth.

79
00:06:14,708 --> 00:06:20,774
Concept five, time-boxing. Clarify requirements: five to eight minutes, ending with eight to twelve

80
00:06:20,774 --> 00:06:22,941
lines plus out of scope.

81
00:06:23,441 --> 00:06:26,518
Actors and use cases: three to five minutes.

82
00:06:27,018 --> 00:06:29,200
Find the classes: eight to ten minutes.

83
00:06:29,700 --> 00:06:34,507
Connect them: eight to ten minutes, with multiplicity, meaning how many of each.

84
00:06:35,007 --> 00:06:39,704
Code: twelve to fifteen minutes for two or three methods. Up to a quarter

85
00:06:39,704 --> 00:06:44,402
of the hour passes before you name a class. Nobody finishes the whole product.

86
00:06:44,402 --> 00:06:47,086
You're judged on the part you built properly.

87
00:06:47,986 --> 00:06:52,674
Machine coding: five to ten minutes reading, then requirements as a comment at the

88
00:06:52,674 --> 00:06:54,349
top of Main dot java.

89
00:06:54,849 --> 00:06:59,782
Ten to fifteen minutes modelling entities, enums and interfaces that compile. An enum is

90
00:06:59,782 --> 00:07:04,011
a type with a fixed list of values, like compact and large.

91
00:07:04,511 --> 00:07:08,829
Thirty to forty minutes on one happy path end to end, meaning the main

92
00:07:08,829 --> 00:07:12,221
flow with valid input. Park a car, exit, print the fee.

93
00:07:12,721 --> 00:07:15,949
Fifteen to twenty on remaining features and edge cases.

94
00:07:16,449 --> 00:07:18,690
Five to ten on polish and a demo script.

95
00:07:19,190 --> 00:07:23,824
And five minutes buffer, never zero. Get something running by halfway.

96
00:07:24,724 --> 00:07:30,740
Concept six, what's scored. One: did you bound the problem? Saying reservations are out

97
00:07:30,740 --> 00:07:32,459
beats a sprawling design.

98
00:07:32,959 --> 00:07:38,791
Two: one job per class, in one sentence. If not, it's two classes glued

99
00:07:38,791 --> 00:07:39,207
together.

100
00:07:39,707 --> 00:07:44,953
Three: right relationships. A lot owns its floors, a ticket only points at a

101
00:07:44,953 --> 00:07:45,328
spot.

102
00:07:45,828 --> 00:07:48,382
Four: does the code match the diagram?

103
00:07:48,882 --> 00:07:55,289
Five: can you explain a trade-off? Machine coding adds: it runs, it's modular, it

104
00:07:55,289 --> 00:08:00,323
handles bad input. Perfect U-M-L isn't graded. The model is graded.

105
00:08:01,223 --> 00:08:06,552
A parking lot has about twelve requirements, twelve classes and fifteen relationships.

106
00:08:07,052 --> 00:08:12,092
Only three carry the main flows: issue a ticket, hold a vehicle, price the

107
00:08:12,092 --> 00:08:17,132
stay. Write those properly, name the rest in a sentence. Writing all twelve at

108
00:08:17,132 --> 00:08:19,292
equal depth is the costliest mistake.

109
00:08:20,192 --> 00:08:26,035
Concept seven. Before any more theory, let's see a complete answer. The question: design

110
00:08:26,035 --> 00:08:31,879
a music playlist. It's small on purpose, but the bigger case studies later follow

111
00:08:31,879 --> 00:08:33,131
the identical outline.

112
00:08:34,031 --> 00:08:40,424
Step one. Three clarifying questions give three requirements. Songs sit in a playlist, and

113
00:08:40,424 --> 00:08:41,337
order matters.

114
00:08:41,837 --> 00:08:45,308
You can ask for the next song, so the player tracks a position.

115
00:08:45,808 --> 00:08:47,747
Shuffle changes the play order.

116
00:08:48,247 --> 00:08:54,679
Out of scope, said out loud: accounts, downloads, storage, the audio. That list earns

117
00:08:54,679 --> 00:08:55,598
credit too.

118
00:08:56,498 --> 00:09:01,951
Step two, the only actor is the listener: add a song, play next, switch

119
00:09:01,951 --> 00:09:05,068
shuffle on. Actors reveal which classes must exist.

120
00:09:05,568 --> 00:09:11,747
Step three, classes. Song holds title, artist and length. Playlist holds songs in order,

121
00:09:11,747 --> 00:09:13,512
it doesn't play them.

122
00:09:14,012 --> 00:09:19,550
PlayOrder is an interface, a set of methods with no implementation. Its job: pick

123
00:09:19,550 --> 00:09:20,736
the next position.

124
00:09:21,236 --> 00:09:26,706
InOrder walks positions in order. Shuffled uses an order decided once, up front.

125
00:09:27,206 --> 00:09:31,870
Player holds a playlist, an order and the current position. Key insight: a rule

126
00:09:31,870 --> 00:09:36,534
that can vary becomes its own type, and Player never knows which version it

127
00:09:36,534 --> 00:09:36,867
holds.

128
00:09:37,767 --> 00:09:42,791
Step four, connect them. Three facts an interviewer checks. The arrow from Playlist to

129
00:09:42,791 --> 00:09:47,815
Song says owns many: the playlist owns its songs, and they go when it

130
00:09:47,815 --> 00:09:52,839
goes. That's called composition. In the book's U-M-L diagram it's drawn as a filled

131
00:09:52,839 --> 00:09:54,992
diamond, with a star meaning many.

132
00:09:55,492 --> 00:10:00,438
The arrow from Player to PlayOrder says uses: the player relies on an ordering

133
00:10:00,438 --> 00:10:05,385
without owning it. And notice it touches only the interface, never InOrder or Shuffled.

134
00:10:05,885 --> 00:10:11,182
The dashed arrows say InOrder and Shuffled implement PlayOrder. In U-M-L that's a dashed

135
00:10:11,182 --> 00:10:16,479
line with a hollow triangle. Add a third rule later, and it's one extra

136
00:10:16,479 --> 00:10:18,371
box. Nothing already drawn changes.

137
00:10:19,271 --> 00:10:24,551
Step five, the code, explained in words. PlayOrder has two methods: first, which gives

138
00:10:24,551 --> 00:10:29,830
the first position to play, and after, which gives the position after the current

139
00:10:29,830 --> 00:10:35,110
one. Both return a constant called NONE, meaning minus one, when there's nothing left.

140
00:10:35,610 --> 00:10:40,528
InOrder's first returns zero unless the list is empty. Its after returns current plus

141
00:10:40,528 --> 00:10:42,636
one, or NONE at the end.

142
00:10:43,136 --> 00:10:48,345
Shuffled is given its order as a list, say positions two, zero, one, instead

143
00:10:48,345 --> 00:10:53,554
of calling a random generator. So the output is identical every run, which makes

144
00:10:53,554 --> 00:10:58,762
it testable. In production you'd inject a random generator with a fixed seed. Inject

145
00:10:58,762 --> 00:11:02,483
means hand it in from outside, usually through the constructor.

146
00:11:02,983 --> 00:11:07,706
Player's playNext asks the order for first on the first call, and for after

147
00:11:07,706 --> 00:11:12,429
on later calls. At the end, it stays at the end. Notice: nothing in

148
00:11:12,429 --> 00:11:17,152
Player tests which mode is active. Its only if statements track where it is.

149
00:11:17,652 --> 00:11:23,259
Song is a Java record: an immutable data carrier whose constructor and accessors are

150
00:11:23,259 --> 00:11:28,866
generated, and its constructor rejects a blank title or a non-positive length. Playlist keeps

151
00:11:28,866 --> 00:11:34,072
its list private. That's encapsulation: data reachable only through the class's own methods.

152
00:11:34,972 --> 00:11:40,669
Here's the real run. Three songs: Sunrise by Ada Lane, Blue Line by The

153
00:11:40,669 --> 00:11:45,552
Meridians, Paper Boats by Nori Kim. In order, it plays Sunrise first.

154
00:11:46,052 --> 00:11:47,399
Then Blue Line.

155
00:11:47,899 --> 00:11:51,022
Then Paper Boats, and then it says end of playlist.

156
00:11:51,522 --> 00:11:57,247
Now the same Player class with Shuffled, given positions two, zero, one. Paper Boats

157
00:11:57,247 --> 00:12:00,928
plays first, because position two is the third song.

158
00:12:01,428 --> 00:12:06,582
Then position zero, Sunrise, then position one, Blue Line, then end of playlist. One

159
00:12:06,582 --> 00:12:11,368
Player class, two behaviours, and not a single if statement about the mode.

160
00:12:12,268 --> 00:12:18,044
One requirement became a type: shuffle produced an interface and two classes. Classes come

161
00:12:18,044 --> 00:12:19,282
out of requirements.

162
00:12:19,782 --> 00:12:21,279
One job per class.

163
00:12:21,779 --> 00:12:27,145
Open to changes you didn't build: repeat one is a new class. That's Strategy

164
00:12:27,145 --> 00:12:28,677
plus the open-closed principle.

165
00:12:29,177 --> 00:12:33,055
Announce the gaps yourself: pause, volume, queue.

166
00:12:33,955 --> 00:12:39,147
Pause and think. The interviewer asks for a repeat one mode, where the same

167
00:12:39,147 --> 00:12:44,340
song plays forever. Which class do you change? Player, Playlist, or none of them?

168
00:12:45,240 --> 00:12:51,595
Answer: change none. Add a RepeatOne class whose after returns the current position. Concept

169
00:12:51,595 --> 00:12:55,680
eight: the full framework, five steps expanded into eleven.

170
00:12:56,580 --> 00:13:02,387
Steps one and two: clarify and write requirements. Who uses it? One location or

171
00:13:02,387 --> 00:13:08,194
many? What must it do, and how well, for example thread-safe, extensible, in memory?

172
00:13:08,194 --> 00:13:12,342
Output: eight to twelve numbered lines plus out of scope.

173
00:13:12,842 --> 00:13:18,749
Step three, use cases: for each actor, what can they do? Customer takes a

174
00:13:18,749 --> 00:13:20,436
ticket, parks, pays, exits.

175
00:13:20,936 --> 00:13:25,997
Step four, entities: which nouns hold state or behaviour, and which are just values?

176
00:13:25,997 --> 00:13:29,251
Spot is a class, spot size is an enum.

177
00:13:29,751 --> 00:13:35,042
Steps five and six, relationships and the class diagram: uses, has, owns, or is-a,

178
00:13:35,042 --> 00:13:40,334
and how many. A lot has one or more floors, a floor has many

179
00:13:40,334 --> 00:13:40,712
spots.

180
00:13:41,212 --> 00:13:46,336
Step seven, key flows: step by step for the two or three main use

181
00:13:46,336 --> 00:13:51,459
cases, and which states the core object moves through. A ticket goes active, then

182
00:13:51,459 --> 00:13:51,825
closed.

183
00:13:52,325 --> 00:13:57,409
Step eight, patterns and SOLID: is there a rule that varies? Pricing, so Strategy.

184
00:13:57,409 --> 00:14:00,676
Is there a class with two reasons to change?

185
00:14:01,176 --> 00:14:06,748
Steps nine and ten, code, then edge cases and concurrency. Two cars grabbing the

186
00:14:06,748 --> 00:14:12,320
same spot? Exit before entry? Use exceptions and locks. An exception is Java's error

187
00:14:12,320 --> 00:14:17,892
signal that stops the normal flow, a lock lets one thread at a time

188
00:14:17,892 --> 00:14:19,086
into protected code.

189
00:14:19,586 --> 00:14:24,977
Step eleven, extensions: what changes if requirement X is added? Short answers, maybe one

190
00:14:24,977 --> 00:14:30,368
extra class. Say each step out loud as you enter it. Your progress being

191
00:14:30,368 --> 00:14:31,909
visible is itself scored.

192
00:14:32,809 --> 00:14:37,208
Spend eight to thirteen minutes on steps one to three, not coding at minute

193
00:14:37,208 --> 00:14:37,522
two.

194
00:14:38,022 --> 00:14:42,249
Code two or three flows fully, rather than stubbing twelve classes.

195
00:14:42,749 --> 00:14:45,906
Name one or two patterns with a signal, not six.

196
00:14:46,406 --> 00:14:52,171
Pass time into methods and hold money as BigDecimal, Java's exact decimal, or cents.

197
00:14:52,171 --> 00:14:56,288
A double can't hold ten cents exactly, so sums drift.

198
00:14:57,188 --> 00:15:02,205
Concept nine. Design a parking lot is four words. The design is about twelve

199
00:15:02,205 --> 00:15:06,504
classes. How do you get from one to the other without guessing?

200
00:15:07,404 --> 00:15:13,401
A requirement states one testable capability. Functional ones are behaviours: a customer takes a

201
00:15:13,401 --> 00:15:14,686
ticket at entry.

202
00:15:15,186 --> 00:15:18,216
The system refuses entry when no spot fits.

203
00:15:18,716 --> 00:15:24,569
Non-functional ones are qualities. Two panels never assign the same spot: thread safety.

204
00:15:25,069 --> 00:15:30,979
A new vehicle type edits no existing class: extensibility. Without a list, the design

205
00:15:30,979 --> 00:15:32,246
has no edges.

206
00:15:33,146 --> 00:15:40,199
Four questions, about five minutes. Who uses this? The actors: customer, attendant, admin, and

207
00:15:40,199 --> 00:15:41,206
the system.

208
00:15:41,706 --> 00:15:46,933
What does each do? Each action is a use case: take ticket, park, pay.

209
00:15:46,933 --> 00:15:49,546
Most of the list comes from here.

210
00:15:50,046 --> 00:15:56,122
What are the limits? Floors, spot kinds, rates. Limits become constants and checks.

211
00:15:56,622 --> 00:16:01,458
What's out of scope? The only question that makes the problem smaller. If they

212
00:16:01,458 --> 00:16:04,566
say your choice, choose, say it, write it down.

213
00:16:05,466 --> 00:16:10,321
Here's what good looks like. Floors with many spots, and five spot types.

214
00:16:10,821 --> 00:16:13,898
Five vehicle types, and a ticket at the entrance panel.

215
00:16:14,398 --> 00:16:18,507
Pay at an exit panel, a portal or an attendant, by cash or card.

216
00:16:19,007 --> 00:16:23,406
An hourly rate that depends on stay length, and each floor showing free spots

217
00:16:23,406 --> 00:16:24,035
by type.

218
00:16:24,535 --> 00:16:30,026
Refuse entry when nothing fits, and admins add floors, spots and update the rate.

219
00:16:30,926 --> 00:16:36,608
Out of scope: season passes, plate recognition, booking ahead, refunds. If no line asked

220
00:16:36,608 --> 00:16:39,450
for it, it's not in the design.

221
00:16:39,950 --> 00:16:44,789
Put a number on each line. Book some seats becomes: at most ten seats

222
00:16:44,789 --> 00:16:48,591
per show, released after ten minutes. A constant and a check.

223
00:16:49,091 --> 00:16:53,837
Name the actor: a member can reserve, so you know which class owns the

224
00:16:53,837 --> 00:16:54,176
method.

225
00:16:54,676 --> 00:16:59,585
Declining is strong: let me park reservations and get ticketing working end to end

226
00:16:59,585 --> 00:16:59,935
first.

227
00:17:00,835 --> 00:17:06,825
A use case is one goal an actor achieves. A list is enough. Customer:

228
00:17:06,825 --> 00:17:08,536
ticket, park, pay, exit.

229
00:17:09,036 --> 00:17:11,579
Attendant: collect cash, help.

230
00:17:12,079 --> 00:17:16,281
Admin: add floors, spots, panels, change the rate.

231
00:17:16,781 --> 00:17:22,993
System: assign spots, update boards, refuse when full. Every class you create traces back

232
00:17:22,993 --> 00:17:25,212
to one of these lines.

233
00:17:26,112 --> 00:17:31,958
Concept ten: finding the classes. A ten-minute method. Collect candidates, cut the ones that

234
00:17:31,958 --> 00:17:35,299
aren't classes, and give each survivor one job.

235
00:17:36,199 --> 00:17:43,062
Step one: underline the nouns. Lot, floor, spot, compact spot, panel, display board.

236
00:17:43,562 --> 00:17:53,188
Car, van, truck, customer, attendant, ticket, payment, cash, card, rate, hour. About twenty-two candidates.

237
00:17:53,688 --> 00:18:00,238
Step two: underline the verbs. Park, pay, leave, refuse entry, show free spots. Each

238
00:18:00,238 --> 00:18:02,109
verb becomes a method.

239
00:18:02,609 --> 00:18:05,836
A verb with no class to live on means a class is missing.

240
00:18:06,736 --> 00:18:11,527
Step three: cut with five tests. Test one: is it a value rather than

241
00:18:11,527 --> 00:18:16,318
a thing? An hour is a number, a licence plate is a string, and

242
00:18:16,318 --> 00:18:21,109
cash or card are options of a payment type. Those become fields or enum

243
00:18:21,109 --> 00:18:21,451
constants.

244
00:18:21,951 --> 00:18:27,037
Test two: is it just another name for a candidate already listed? Customer and

245
00:18:27,037 --> 00:18:31,034
driver are the same actor. Merge them and pick one name.

246
00:18:31,534 --> 00:18:37,353
Test three: does it have state that changes? A spot is free, then occupied.

247
00:18:37,353 --> 00:18:41,094
An hour never changes. Changing state means a class.

248
00:18:41,594 --> 00:18:47,165
Test four: does anything call it? Nobody asks an Hour object a question. Never

249
00:18:47,165 --> 00:18:49,155
called means it's a value.

250
00:18:49,655 --> 00:18:55,152
Test five, the big one: different behaviour, or only different data? Compact and large

251
00:18:55,152 --> 00:19:00,648
spots differ only in which vehicle fits. That's data, so one ParkingSpot class with

252
00:19:00,648 --> 00:19:06,145
a type field. Cash checks the amount handed over, while card calls a provider

253
00:19:06,145 --> 00:19:11,641
and waits. That's behaviour, so two classes. Tests one to four cut twenty-two down

254
00:19:11,641 --> 00:19:15,960
to about fifteen, test five alone gets you to about twelve.

255
00:19:16,860 --> 00:19:22,877
Step four, one sentence each. ParkingLot issues tickets and knows when it's full. ParkingFloor

256
00:19:22,877 --> 00:19:24,596
finds an empty spot.

257
00:19:25,096 --> 00:19:30,113
ParkingSpot tracks its vehicle. Vehicle carries its plate and fitting spot types.

258
00:19:30,613 --> 00:19:36,257
Ticket notes vehicle, spot and entry time. ParkingRate turns a duration into a price.

259
00:19:36,757 --> 00:19:42,506
Payment collects money. The board shows free counts. ParkingLot's sentence has an and. Naming

260
00:19:42,506 --> 00:19:44,970
that tension out loud is enough.

261
00:19:45,870 --> 00:19:51,083
Step five: put each method where its data lives. Find a free spot goes

262
00:19:51,083 --> 00:19:51,828
on ParkingFloor.

263
00:19:52,328 --> 00:19:58,703
Pricing goes on ParkingRate, taking a ticket on ParkingLot. That's the Information Expert guideline.

264
00:19:59,203 --> 00:20:04,347
Mistake: one subclass per value, like CompactSpot with no behaviour.

265
00:20:04,847 --> 00:20:09,200
Mistake: missing the record keeper, a ticket, and the rule, a rate.

266
00:20:09,700 --> 00:20:12,928
Mistake: a Manager god class owning everything.

267
00:20:13,828 --> 00:20:19,339
Pause and think. Car, van and truck: should they be three subclasses of Vehicle,

268
00:20:19,339 --> 00:20:21,307
or one enum called VehicleType?

269
00:20:22,207 --> 00:20:29,017
Answer: an enum, if they differ only in which spots fit. Concept eleven, connecting

270
00:20:29,017 --> 00:20:30,476
classes. Five choices.

271
00:20:30,976 --> 00:20:37,073
Dependency: briefly uses, like the lot calling the rate. Association: holds a reference, like

272
00:20:37,073 --> 00:20:43,171
a ticket and its spot. Aggregation: contains parts that live alone. Composition: owns parts

273
00:20:43,171 --> 00:20:46,655
that die with it, like floor and spots.

274
00:20:47,155 --> 00:20:52,428
Inheritance: is a kind of. Then add multiplicity. The classic error is inheritance where

275
00:20:52,428 --> 00:20:57,702
composition is right. A car is not an engine, so Car has an Engine.

276
00:20:58,602 --> 00:21:03,784
Concept twelve. SOLID is five rules, one per letter. Run them as five quick

277
00:21:03,784 --> 00:21:07,115
checks on your own diagram, in about five minutes.

278
00:21:08,015 --> 00:21:14,208
S, single responsibility: one reason to change. The signal it failed: the class sentence

279
00:21:14,208 --> 00:21:20,401
needs and. Our ParkingLot issues tickets and finds spots. Finding: split it if time

280
00:21:20,401 --> 00:21:23,055
allows, otherwise say it out loud.

281
00:21:23,555 --> 00:21:29,277
O, open-closed: add behaviour without editing existing code. The signal: a switch or if

282
00:21:29,277 --> 00:21:35,000
chain over a type. Our pricing sits behind ParkingRate, so a weekend rate is

283
00:21:35,000 --> 00:21:38,270
a new class, not a switch edit. Passes.

284
00:21:38,770 --> 00:21:44,566
L, Liskov substitution: a subclass must work anywhere its parent does. The signal: a

285
00:21:44,566 --> 00:21:50,362
subclass that throws or does nothing. A ReadOnlySpot whose occupy method throws would fail.

286
00:21:50,362 --> 00:21:55,331
Read every inheritance arrow at the end, since they get added late.

287
00:21:55,831 --> 00:22:03,179
I, interface segregation: no class implements methods it doesn't use. The signal: empty method

288
00:22:03,179 --> 00:22:06,854
bodies. We have no wide interfaces. Passes.

289
00:22:07,354 --> 00:22:13,474
D, dependency inversion: depend on an interface, not a concrete class. The signal: a

290
00:22:13,474 --> 00:22:19,595
business class constructs a technical one itself. If the lot did new CreditCardTransaction inside,

291
00:22:19,595 --> 00:22:25,715
you couldn't test it or add cash. It depends on a Payment interface instead.

292
00:22:25,715 --> 00:22:31,836
Passes. Two real findings is a good pass. Five means rework. Zero means you

293
00:22:31,836 --> 00:22:32,710
didn't look.

294
00:22:33,610 --> 00:22:38,797
S and O earn most credit. What if trucks are charged differently? Add a

295
00:22:38,797 --> 00:22:40,649
class, not a switch branch.

296
00:22:41,149 --> 00:22:45,829
Don't make an interface for everything. One implementer means pointless indirection.

297
00:22:46,329 --> 00:22:50,664
Add one for a second implementation or a test double, a fake used in

298
00:22:50,664 --> 00:22:50,973
tests.

299
00:22:51,473 --> 00:22:55,156
And don't over-split. The one-sentence test is the line.

300
00:22:56,056 --> 00:23:01,474
Concept thirteen. There are twenty-three classic Gang of Four patterns. Six recur here. A

301
00:23:01,474 --> 00:23:06,893
misapplied pattern hurts more than an unknown one, so learn each with its signal.

302
00:23:07,793 --> 00:23:14,152
Strategy. Signal: one operation with several interchangeable rules. Parking lot: ParkingRate with HourlyRate, WeekendRate

303
00:23:14,152 --> 00:23:20,512
and MonthlyPass. Without it, the pricing method grows a switch you edit for every

304
00:23:20,512 --> 00:23:24,146
rate. Skip it if there's only one rule.

305
00:23:24,646 --> 00:23:29,876
Factory. Signal: what you create depends on a type value, and the caller shouldn't

306
00:23:29,876 --> 00:23:35,106
know the concrete class. A factory turns a vehicle type into a Vehicle, so

307
00:23:35,106 --> 00:23:40,336
the entrance panel doesn't know every vehicle class. A factory method is usually enough.

308
00:23:40,836 --> 00:23:46,532
Observer. Signal: one thing changes and several others must know, without the source knowing

309
00:23:46,532 --> 00:23:52,228
them. The spot is the subject. Display boards and panels register and update when

310
00:23:52,228 --> 00:23:56,296
it fills. With only one listener, just call it directly.

311
00:23:56,796 --> 00:24:02,461
State. Signal: behaviour depends on the current state, with a fixed set of transitions.

312
00:24:02,461 --> 00:24:08,125
A ticket goes active, paid, closed. Choose it only for big transition tables. For

313
00:24:08,125 --> 00:24:13,790
three or four states, an enum plus one guarded method is enough, and say

314
00:24:13,790 --> 00:24:14,194
why.

315
00:24:14,694 --> 00:24:21,804
Singleton. Signal: exactly one instance must exist, like the ParkingLot. Expect two follow-ups every

316
00:24:21,804 --> 00:24:28,913
time: thread safety, and global state hurting tests. Many teams inject one shared instance

317
00:24:28,913 --> 00:24:29,421
instead.

318
00:24:29,921 --> 00:24:35,687
Builder. Signal: many optional fields and a long constructor. A library BookItem with eight

319
00:24:35,687 --> 00:24:41,454
arguments is easy to mix up, since two adjacent strings compile fine. A builder

320
00:24:41,454 --> 00:24:46,808
sets fields by name. Mention it at about six arguments and move on.

321
00:24:47,708 --> 00:24:50,634
Strategy: a Comparator passed to List dot sort.

322
00:24:51,134 --> 00:24:55,430
Factory: List dot of, and Executors' thread pool methods.

323
00:24:55,930 --> 00:24:59,227
Observer: Swing's ActionListener, Spring events.

324
00:24:59,727 --> 00:25:03,605
State: an order moving placed, paid, shipped, delivered.

325
00:25:04,105 --> 00:25:06,566
Singleton: Runtime dot getRuntime.

326
00:25:07,066 --> 00:25:13,166
Builder: StringBuilder. Use one or two patterns, and compare: rate gets Strategy, ticket doesn't

327
00:25:13,166 --> 00:25:16,217
get State, because one guard is enough.

328
00:25:17,117 --> 00:25:24,834
Concept fourteen, interview code in five layers. Models: entities like ParkingSpot, records like Vehicle,

329
00:25:24,834 --> 00:25:26,487
enums like VehicleType.

330
00:25:26,987 --> 00:25:32,553
Repositories store and find objects in an in-memory map. Strategies hold the rules that

331
00:25:32,553 --> 00:25:33,746
vary, like pricing.

332
00:25:34,246 --> 00:25:39,351
Services are the use cases, park and exit. The driver Main runs a scripted

333
00:25:39,351 --> 00:25:39,716
demo.

334
00:25:40,616 --> 00:25:44,981
Pass time in: park takes now minute as a parameter, so it's testable.

335
00:25:45,481 --> 00:25:48,407
Money as BigDecimal or cents, never double.

336
00:25:48,907 --> 00:25:51,020
Fail loudly with clear exceptions.

337
00:25:51,520 --> 00:25:56,620
Return an Optional, a box that's full or empty, when not found is normal.

338
00:25:56,620 --> 00:25:58,441
Park returns empty when full.

339
00:25:58,941 --> 00:26:02,041
Fields private and final, and domain names.

340
00:26:02,941 --> 00:26:06,993
Input: null, negative, an unknown ticket ID at exit.

341
00:26:07,493 --> 00:26:10,477
Capacity: the lot is full for this vehicle type.

342
00:26:10,977 --> 00:26:14,286
State: exiting twice, exiting without entering.

343
00:26:14,786 --> 00:26:18,768
Time: exit before entry, and do part hours round up?

344
00:26:19,268 --> 00:26:25,559
Concurrency: two panels grab one spot. Use synchronized or a lock, or atomic structures.

345
00:26:25,559 --> 00:26:30,951
Lock the smallest thing that keeps the invariant: one spot, one vehicle.

346
00:26:31,851 --> 00:26:36,998
Concept fifteen. Now let's run every step of the framework end to end, on

347
00:26:36,998 --> 00:26:41,409
a deliberately small parking lot. Watch how each step feeds the next.

348
00:26:42,309 --> 00:26:46,421
Single lot or many? One lot, one or more floors.

349
00:26:46,921 --> 00:26:49,812
Vehicles: motorbike, car, truck.

350
00:26:50,312 --> 00:26:56,327
Spots: motorbike, compact, large. Smaller vehicles may use bigger spots.

351
00:26:56,827 --> 00:26:59,881
Payment is out of scope: we compute the fee only.

352
00:27:00,381 --> 00:27:04,444
Pricing: tiered hourly, round up, trucks double, swappable.

353
00:27:04,944 --> 00:27:07,638
Concurrency: several panels at once.

354
00:27:08,538 --> 00:27:13,043
Requirement one: floors with motorbike, compact and large spots.

355
00:27:13,543 --> 00:27:19,567
Two: a motorbike fits motorbike or compact, a car compact or large, a truck

356
00:27:19,567 --> 00:27:21,288
large only. Smallest first.

357
00:27:21,788 --> 00:27:26,599
Three and four: on entry, issue a ticket for a free fitting spot, or

358
00:27:26,599 --> 00:27:26,943
refuse.

359
00:27:27,443 --> 00:27:32,704
Five and six: on exit, free the spot and return the fee. Four dollars

360
00:27:32,704 --> 00:27:37,965
first hour, three fifty for hours two and three, two fifty after. Round up.

361
00:27:37,965 --> 00:27:39,093
Trucks pay double.

362
00:27:39,593 --> 00:27:45,618
Seven and eight: swappable pricing, and free counts per floor. Plus thread-safe, in memory,

363
00:27:45,618 --> 00:27:46,909
time passed in.

364
00:27:47,809 --> 00:27:53,019
Use cases: the driver enters and gets a ticket, then exits and sees the

365
00:27:53,019 --> 00:27:58,228
fee. The admin adds floors and spots and changes the pricing rule. The system

366
00:27:58,228 --> 00:28:03,438
finds a free spot, refuses when full and computes the fee. Now the entities.

367
00:28:03,438 --> 00:28:06,043
SpotType is an enum of spot sizes.

368
00:28:06,543 --> 00:28:11,942
VehicleType is an enum, and each value knows which spot types fit, smallest first.

369
00:28:12,442 --> 00:28:15,913
Vehicle is a record: a plate and a type, pure data.

370
00:28:16,413 --> 00:28:20,848
ParkingSpot is a class that tracks whether it's occupied and by which vehicle.

371
00:28:21,348 --> 00:28:25,191
ParkingFloor holds the spots on one floor and finds a free fitting one.

372
00:28:25,691 --> 00:28:31,056
Ticket is a record: which vehicle went into which spot, and when. It's the

373
00:28:31,056 --> 00:28:33,738
connection object, the record keeper beginners forget.

374
00:28:34,238 --> 00:28:40,396
PricingStrategy is an interface that turns a stay into a fee, with two implementations:

375
00:28:40,396 --> 00:28:41,715
HourlyRate and FlatWeekendRate.

376
00:28:42,215 --> 00:28:47,975
And ParkingLot issues and closes tickets. It's the service the panels call. Notice spot

377
00:28:47,975 --> 00:28:52,911
sizes are an enum, not three subclasses: different data means a field.

378
00:28:53,811 --> 00:28:56,865
The lot owns its floors, each floor owns its spots.

379
00:28:57,365 --> 00:29:01,533
The lot keeps active tickets, each pointing at a vehicle and a spot.

380
00:29:02,033 --> 00:29:06,038
The lot depends only on PricingStrategy, with two implementations.

381
00:29:06,938 --> 00:29:11,247
The key flow, as a sequence. The entry panel calls park with the vehicle

382
00:29:11,247 --> 00:29:15,556
and the current minute. The lot asks each floor to find a free spot

383
00:29:15,556 --> 00:29:16,787
for that vehicle type.

384
00:29:17,287 --> 00:29:22,389
The floor returns an Optional spot. If a spot is found, the lot occupies

385
00:29:22,389 --> 00:29:27,491
it and returns a ticket. If not, it returns empty, and the panel refuses

386
00:29:27,491 --> 00:29:27,855
entry.

387
00:29:28,355 --> 00:29:33,088
On exit, the lot removes the ticket, releases the spot, then asks the pricing

388
00:29:33,088 --> 00:29:36,807
strategy for the fee using the vehicle type and minutes parked.

389
00:29:37,707 --> 00:29:39,925
Strategy for pricing, not a switch.

390
00:29:40,425 --> 00:29:42,909
Enums for types, not subclasses.

391
00:29:43,409 --> 00:29:46,138
Ticket as a record, since it never changes.

392
00:29:46,638 --> 00:29:50,933
BigDecimal, so three fifty plus three fifty is exactly seven.

393
00:29:51,433 --> 00:29:57,623
Synchronized park and exit, so two panels never share a spot. Per-floor locks later.

394
00:29:58,123 --> 00:30:00,352
No Singleton: inject the lot.

395
00:30:00,852 --> 00:30:03,755
No State pattern: just active and closed.

396
00:30:04,655 --> 00:30:10,685
VehicleType is an enum where each value carries its fitting list. Motorbike fits motorbike

397
00:30:10,685 --> 00:30:15,422
then compact. Car fits compact then large. Truck fits large only.

398
00:30:15,922 --> 00:30:21,140
ParkingSpot has a vehicle field, where null means free. Occupy throws an illegal state

399
00:30:21,140 --> 00:30:26,358
exception if the spot's taken. Occupy and release are package-private, so only the lot

400
00:30:26,358 --> 00:30:27,849
can change a spot.

401
00:30:28,349 --> 00:30:33,310
HourlyRate rounds minutes up to whole hours, adds minutes plus fifty-nine and divides by

402
00:30:33,310 --> 00:30:38,270
sixty, minimum one. Then it adds four dollars for hour one, three fifty for

403
00:30:38,270 --> 00:30:43,230
hours two and three, and two fifty after. For a truck, it doubles the

404
00:30:43,230 --> 00:30:43,585
total.

405
00:30:44,085 --> 00:30:49,098
ParkingLot's park loops over floors, finds a spot, occupies it, creates ticket T one,

406
00:30:49,098 --> 00:30:54,111
T two and so on, stores it in the active map and returns it.

407
00:30:54,111 --> 00:30:57,333
If no floor has a spot, it returns empty.

408
00:30:57,833 --> 00:31:02,929
Exit looks up the ticket, throws if it's unknown or if exit is before

409
00:31:02,929 --> 00:31:08,024
entry, and only then removes it, releases the spot and returns the fee. Validate

410
00:31:08,024 --> 00:31:09,480
first, then change state.

411
00:31:10,380 --> 00:31:15,277
The demo: one floor with one motorbike, one compact and one large spot. A

412
00:31:15,277 --> 00:31:20,174
car parks at minute zero and gets ticket T one, spot F zero dash

413
00:31:20,174 --> 00:31:21,574
C one, the compact.

414
00:31:22,074 --> 00:31:26,694
A truck parks at minute ten and gets T two, the only large spot.

415
00:31:27,194 --> 00:31:32,518
A second car at minute twenty is refused. Compact and large are both taken.

416
00:31:32,518 --> 00:31:34,419
That's requirement four in action.

417
00:31:34,919 --> 00:31:37,230
The free compact count prints zero.

418
00:31:37,730 --> 00:31:42,667
T one exits at minute one fifty. A hundred and fifty minutes rounds up

419
00:31:42,667 --> 00:31:47,251
to three hours: four dollars plus three fifty plus three fifty, eleven dollars.

420
00:31:47,751 --> 00:31:52,431
The truck exits at minute sixty, so fifty minutes rounds up to one hour,

421
00:31:52,431 --> 00:31:54,102
four dollars, doubled to eight.

422
00:31:54,602 --> 00:31:59,612
Then we swap in the weekend strategy at run time. The second car now

423
00:31:59,612 --> 00:32:04,622
parks, and exits paying a flat ten dollars. Exiting T one again throws: unknown

424
00:32:04,622 --> 00:32:06,053
or already closed ticket.

425
00:32:06,953 --> 00:32:12,263
Why synchronized? Park and exit lock the whole lot, so find-then-occupy is atomic. Without

426
00:32:12,263 --> 00:32:17,573
the lock, two panels could both see the compact spot free and both take

427
00:32:17,573 --> 00:32:22,883
it. That's a check-then-act race condition, a bug whose result depends on which thread

428
00:32:22,883 --> 00:32:23,641
runs first.

429
00:32:24,141 --> 00:32:29,029
FreeSpots reads under the same lock, so a display never sees a half-finished park.

430
00:32:29,529 --> 00:32:34,618
Exit validates the ticket and the time before changing anything, so a rejected exit

431
00:32:34,618 --> 00:32:39,706
leaves the ticket open. Exit before entry, an unknown ticket and a double exit

432
00:32:39,706 --> 00:32:40,433
all throw.

433
00:32:40,933 --> 00:32:45,588
And occupy also throws if the spot's taken: a second line of defence.

434
00:32:46,488 --> 00:32:52,202
Electric spots? New enum values, and a ChargingSpot if charging has behaviour.

435
00:32:52,702 --> 00:32:56,918
Nearest spot? A SpotSelectionStrategy, injected.

436
00:32:57,418 --> 00:32:59,684
Update boards? Observer.

437
00:33:00,184 --> 00:33:04,771
More panels? Lock per floor, or a thread-safe queue of free spots.

438
00:33:05,271 --> 00:33:11,289
Cash or card? A Payment interface and a payment service. That's dependency inversion.

439
00:33:12,189 --> 00:33:16,997
Concept sixteen, communication. Think out loud and name your step.

440
00:33:17,497 --> 00:33:19,680
Write things down and confirm scope.

441
00:33:20,180 --> 00:33:24,092
State assumptions, and give trade-offs as X over Y, because.

442
00:33:24,592 --> 00:33:27,112
Welcome hints, and say what you're leaving out.

443
00:33:27,612 --> 00:33:30,119
Watch the clock and announce the move to code.

444
00:33:30,619 --> 00:33:35,893
Use domain names, and when stuck, go back to the requirements. Never go silent

445
00:33:35,893 --> 00:33:36,647
for minutes.

446
00:33:37,547 --> 00:33:44,319
Concept seventeen. Reading case studies builds little skill. Attempt first, then compare.

447
